The origin of COVID-19 remains disputed between two main hypotheses. Proponents of zoonosis argue that the earliest known cases cluster tightly around the Huanan Seafood Market, which they take as strong evidence that the virus spilled over from animals sold there. Critics respond that this clustering may reflect where testing and surveillance were concentrated rather than where the outbreak began, so the market pattern is not decisive. Supporters of a lab-leak hypothesis note that the Wuhan Institute of Virology conducted research on bat coronaviruses nearby, which they argue raises the prior probability of an accidental escape. Others counter that proximity alone establishes opportunity, not occurrence, and that no direct evidence of an infected lab worker has been produced. A separate line of argument holds that the furin cleavage site in the virus is unusual, which some treat as a signature of engineering; virologists reply that similar features occur in natural coronaviruses, so the feature does not by itself favor a laboratory origin.

